1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,146
That's the first of the four questions a
CTO asks after a security scare, and none

2
00:00:09,186 --> 00:00:13,774
of the four is about a command. Gate 8
asks you questions of that kind and scores

3
00:00:13,814 --> 00:00:18,575
two things that a command can't show: whether
you can say what a credential or a job

4
00:00:18,615 --> 00:00:21,546
could reach, and whether
you act in the right order.

5
00:00:21,586 --> 00:00:25,603
An answer that does the right things
in the wrong order fails the case.

6
00:00:25,643 --> 00:00:28,460
Keep that question in mind.
A terminal answers it.

7
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:34,072
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

8
00:00:34,112 --> 00:00:38,196
This is a briefing for a gate, one
of this course's mastery tests.

9
00:00:38,236 --> 00:00:43,320
It teaches nothing new and it shows no gate
item. It tells you what Gate 8 covers,

10
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:48,382
how it is taken, which two procedures you
need to have ready, and how to prepare.

11
00:00:48,722 --> 00:00:53,125
The gate file and the case files aren't
opened in this video, and you don't open

12
00:00:53,165 --> 00:00:57,513
them before you sit the gate: a gate that
has been read is a gate that has been

13
00:00:57,553 --> 00:00:57,846
taken.

14
00:00:59,686 --> 00:01:01,265
Here's what you'll walk away with.

15
00:01:01,605 --> 00:01:03,017
After this video you can:

16
00:01:03,357 --> 00:01:06,668
State what Gate 8 covers
and its threshold of 90.

17
00:01:07,008 --> 00:01:11,360
Explain the paper format and the rule
never to copy or run its workflow files.

18
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:16,248
Prepare the two procedures: the workflow
review checklist and the six-step leak

19
00:01:16,288 --> 00:01:16,771
response.

20
00:01:17,111 --> 00:01:21,374
Answer with blast radius: what could
this credential or this job reach.

21
00:01:23,214 --> 00:01:27,055
What the gate covers. Gate
8 is taken after Module 31.

22
00:01:27,095 --> 00:01:32,950
It covers Actions security, repository
security and secret-leak response: Chapters

23
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:38,772
21A and 21B. Actions is GitHub's automation,
and a workflow is the file that says

24
00:01:38,812 --> 00:01:43,588
which jobs run when an event happens.
You pass at 90 points of 100.

25
00:01:43,928 --> 00:01:48,085
How it is built. Like every gate it
has four parts with fixed weights.

26
00:01:48,425 --> 00:01:53,648
The pass rule has two conditions: the
threshold overall, and at least 70 percent in

27
00:01:53,688 --> 00:01:54,345
every part.

28
00:01:54,685 --> 00:02:01,590
That is 21 of 30 in Concepts, 14 of 20 in
Prediction, 21 of 30 in the cases, 14 of

29
00:02:01,630 --> 00:02:03,284
20 in the oral part.

30
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:09,276
Quick quiz. Your total is above the
threshold, and your oral part is 13 of 20.

31
00:02:09,316 --> 00:02:11,976
A, a pass, or B, a miss? Your answer?

32
00:02:16,816 --> 00:02:21,154
B. A total above the threshold with
one part below 70 percent is a miss.

33
00:02:21,494 --> 00:02:26,753
Why Part 3 is on paper. The gates for
GitHub, Actions and Security run nothing on

34
00:02:26,793 --> 00:02:32,051
GitHub. No GitHub output appears anywhere
in them, and the only gh invocation you

35
00:02:32,091 --> 00:02:37,546
need is gh <command> --help. The workflow
files in the case directories are teaching

36
00:02:37,586 --> 00:02:42,845
material with faults on purpose. Never
copy them into a repository, and never run

37
00:02:42,885 --> 00:02:46,770
them. Reading a faulty workflow
is the skill being examined.

38
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:50,760
Executing one is the mistake the
chapter taught you to avoid.

39
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:56,137
Order is scored. In a leak-response case,
an answer that rewrites history before

40
00:02:56,177 --> 00:03:00,452
revoking loses the points for the
case, whatever else it gets right.

41
00:03:00,492 --> 00:03:05,783
You know the reason from section 21B.14:
the damage happens at the issuer, where the

42
00:03:05,823 --> 00:03:09,972
key is accepted, and revocation is
the only step that is complete.

43
00:03:10,312 --> 00:03:14,902
After a miss. A miss leads to remediation
and a different variant, not to the

44
00:03:14,942 --> 00:03:19,893
answers. You receive your score sheet,
restudy the sections of the remediation map,

45
00:03:19,933 --> 00:03:24,402
wait at least two days, and retake the
whole gate with variant B in Part 3.

46
00:03:26,242 --> 00:03:30,596
A picture helps. Think of the gate as a
review board for two documents that you

47
00:03:30,636 --> 00:03:31,653
carry in your head.

48
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:35,645
The first document is a checklist
that you lay over any workflow.

49
00:03:35,685 --> 00:03:40,303
The second is a sequence that you run on
any leak. Neither is recited at the gate.

50
00:03:40,343 --> 00:03:44,905
Each is applied to a case you haven't seen,
and each answer is expected to end in

51
00:03:44,945 --> 00:03:49,620
the same kind of sentence: this job, or
this credential, could reach the following,

52
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:51,267
and for the following reason.

53
00:03:51,607 --> 00:03:56,047
Where this model breaks: a checklist
can be applied line by line, and a leak

54
00:03:56,087 --> 00:04:00,821
response cannot. Its steps are ordered
because each one changes what the next one

55
00:04:00,861 --> 00:04:03,356
finds. That's why the
order carries points.

56
00:04:03,696 --> 00:04:09,027
Try it now, on paper, for thirty seconds.
Write the six steps of the leak response

57
00:04:09,067 --> 00:04:11,189
from memory, in order. I'll wait.

58
00:04:17,529 --> 00:04:22,421
The left list first, with what each heading
asks about, in half a sentence: which

59
00:04:22,461 --> 00:04:26,987
event starts the job. Which code is
checked out. What the job token may do.

60
00:04:27,027 --> 00:04:31,553
Whether an expression lands inside a
script. Whether each action is pinned.

61
00:04:31,593 --> 00:04:35,693
Whether code is downloaded and run.
Where each secret is referenced.

62
00:04:35,733 --> 00:04:39,833
Under which conditions a publishing
job runs. Which machine runs it.

63
00:04:39,873 --> 00:04:44,094
Whether credentials stay in the
checkout. And who reviewed the change.

64
00:04:44,434 --> 00:04:49,265
Now the right list, and your paper:
contain, assess, eradicate, recover,

65
00:04:49,305 --> 00:04:52,445
communicate, prevent.
Underline the first step.

66
00:04:54,285 --> 00:04:59,364
A warm-up in the lab, not a gate item.
Assessment, the second response step, needs

67
00:04:59,404 --> 00:05:04,358
two facts that only Git can give: the
first commit that contains the secret, and

68
00:05:04,398 --> 00:05:09,540
which refs, meaning branches and tags,
contain it. Both commands only read history.

69
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:14,720
Step 1: the pickaxe. The repository
deleted its .env file in a later commit.

70
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:20,049
Predict: how many commits will a search for
the string list, and why more than one?

71
00:05:20,089 --> 00:05:21,077
Say it out loud.

72
00:05:25,917 --> 00:05:31,175
Two commits: 0805fd8, which added the
string, and d42d1a1, which removed it.

73
00:05:31,215 --> 00:05:36,960
The pickaxe lists commits that change the
number of occurrences, so the deletion is

74
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:37,727
listed too.

75
00:05:38,067 --> 00:05:42,590
The deletion didn't remove anything from
history. It's one more commit on top.

76
00:05:42,630 --> 00:05:47,504
So, to the opening question: we deleted it,
and no, that alone doesn't make us safe.

77
00:05:47,844 --> 00:05:50,502
Step 2: which refs
contain the first commit.

78
00:05:50,842 --> 00:05:53,355
Look at the tag, the
last line of the output.

79
00:05:55,695 --> 00:06:00,269
A branch listing alone would have missed
it. In a paper case the same evidence is

80
00:06:00,309 --> 00:06:04,827
printed for you, and you're asked what it
means. The warm-up is to say, for each

81
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:08,244
line of such output, what it
rules in and what it rules out.

82
00:06:10,084 --> 00:06:11,732
Five mistakes to watch for.

83
00:06:12,073 --> 00:06:16,549
Opening the gate file "to see what is
in it". Root cause: the gate measures

84
00:06:16,589 --> 00:06:21,427
diagnosis of an unseen case, and a case
whose content has been seen can no longer

85
00:06:21,467 --> 00:06:22,210
measure that.

86
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:26,848
Answering a leak case with the cleanup
first. Root cause: the answer treats the

87
00:06:26,888 --> 00:06:31,077
repository as the place of the damage,
when the damage happens at the issuer.

88
00:06:31,417 --> 00:06:34,709
Reviewing a workflow by looking
for the one familiar fault.

89
00:06:34,749 --> 00:06:39,452
Root cause: the faults interact, and only
a pass over every heading of the checklist

90
00:06:39,492 --> 00:06:41,881
shows which input reaches
which permission.

91
00:06:42,221 --> 00:06:46,946
Naming a control without its layer. Root
cause: a habit, a client setting, a server

92
00:06:46,986 --> 00:06:51,596
rule and a pipeline check fail in different
ways, and an answer that does not say

93
00:06:51,636 --> 00:06:54,467
which one it means cannot
be scored for mechanism.

94
00:06:54,807 --> 00:06:59,676
Passing on the total and missing a part.
Root cause: the pass rule requires 70

95
00:06:59,716 --> 00:07:04,584
percent in each part, so a weak oral part
is not compensated by strong written

96
00:07:04,624 --> 00:07:04,962
parts.

97
00:07:06,802 --> 00:07:11,851
Now, out of the lab. A reviewer on an ML
platform team receives a pull request that

98
00:07:11,891 --> 00:07:16,083
adds an evaluation workflow. She
doesn't start by reading the script.

99
00:07:16,423 --> 00:07:21,171
She goes down the checklist: the trigger,
and which outsider-controlled input

100
00:07:21,211 --> 00:07:24,466
reaches the job. The checkout.
The permissions block.

101
00:07:24,506 --> 00:07:29,379
Expressions inside run:. The pins of the
actions. The secrets and where they're

102
00:07:29,419 --> 00:07:34,415
referenced. The runner. For each finding
she writes one sentence of blast radius:

103
00:07:34,455 --> 00:07:38,519
what the job token could write,
what the provider key could reach.

104
00:07:38,859 --> 00:07:42,110
The same week a colleague
reports a key in a notebook.

105
00:07:42,150 --> 00:07:47,228
She doesn't open the repository first either.
She asks who can revoke the key at the

106
00:07:47,268 --> 00:07:51,981
provider, and has it done, and only then
runs the two commands of the warm-up.

107
00:07:52,021 --> 00:07:55,028
The gate asks for exactly
this behavior, on paper.

108
00:07:56,868 --> 00:08:01,233
Your turn. Redo the three Level
5 exercises of the block in

109
00:08:01,273 --> 00:08:07,205
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md:
Exercise 29.8, "A workflow nobody wrote".

110
00:08:07,245 --> 00:08:13,328
Exercise 30.7, "The reproduction package".
And Exercise 31.7, "Lead the response".

111
00:08:13,368 --> 00:08:19,076
Do them closed book and timed. Before
each one, predict which headings of the

112
00:08:19,116 --> 00:08:24,974
checklist, or which of the six steps, the
exercise will turn on, and check your

113
00:08:25,014 --> 00:08:30,350
prediction afterwards. Then, and not
before, compare with the solutions.

114
00:08:30,690 --> 00:08:35,766
When the three are done: take Gate 8,
assessments/gate-8-security.md, in one

115
00:08:35,806 --> 00:08:37,987
sitting, with the parts in order.

116
00:08:41,827 --> 00:08:46,758
Answer out loud. There's more than one
defensible choice, and the question scores

117
00:08:46,798 --> 00:08:51,667
the reasoning. A strong answer names one
control and the layer that enforces it,

118
00:08:51,707 --> 00:08:56,577
says what it limits in terms of blast
radius, and says what it doesn't cover, so

119
00:08:56,617 --> 00:09:01,732
that the listener knows what remains open.
It also argues from evidence: the pattern

120
00:09:01,772 --> 00:09:04,984
that the case studies of
Chapters 21A and 21B repeat.

121
00:09:05,024 --> 00:09:08,114
An answer that lists five
controls hasn't answered.

122
00:09:08,154 --> 00:09:10,200
The constraint "one" is the point.

123
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:13,864
Let's land this in your own words.

124
00:09:14,204 --> 00:09:19,743
Gate 8 covers Actions security, repository
security and secret-leak response, and is

125
00:09:19,783 --> 00:09:23,197
passed at 90 with at least
70 percent in every part.

126
00:09:23,537 --> 00:09:28,597
Its third part is three cases on paper;
the workflow files in them are faulty on

127
00:09:28,637 --> 00:09:30,892
purpose and are never copied or run.

128
00:09:31,232 --> 00:09:35,943
You bring two procedures: the eleven headings
of the workflow review checklist and

129
00:09:35,983 --> 00:09:37,797
the six response steps in order.

130
00:09:38,137 --> 00:09:42,370
Order is scored: contain first, and
rewrite history only where warranted.

131
00:09:42,710 --> 00:09:47,611
Every answer ends with blast radius: what
this credential or this job could reach.

132
00:09:49,451 --> 00:09:54,543
Before the gate, answer the "Interview
questions" sections of Chapters 21A and 21B

133
00:09:54,583 --> 00:09:59,801
aloud, one question at a time, without notes.
Record yourself if you rehearse alone,

134
00:09:59,841 --> 00:10:02,117
and score the recording the next day.

135
00:10:02,457 --> 00:10:07,541
You have worked through the whole security
part, and you know what the gate asks of

136
00:10:07,581 --> 00:10:12,664
you. Rehearse out loud, then sit it. Next
time, Part 8 begins: the fork workflow in

137
00:10:12,704 --> 00:10:16,491
practice. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

138
00:10:16,531 --> 00:10:17,972
See you in the next one.
